<!-- ABSTRACT -->
<h1>Abstract</h1>
<p>Many companies today use more than one cloud provider, such as Amazon Web Services (AWS), Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud Platform (GCP). While using multiple clouds helps businesses run smoothly, managing the monthly bills is very difficult. Each cloud company uses different names, units, and billing formats for their services. Because of this confusion, developers often leave virtual machines running, forget to delete old storage disks, or run high-cost data transfers without noticing. This leads to heavy financial waste, where companies lose around 20% to 35% of their total cloud budget.</p>
<p>In this project, we built a practical multi-cloud FinOps software system to solve these billing problems. We used the FOCUS 1.0 standard format, which brings all cost data from AWS, Azure, and GCP into one clean, common table. To help companies predict their future expenses, we made a machine learning spend forecasting model. This model looks at daily spending patterns and seasonal trends using Fourier analysis and gradient boosted trees, giving safe upper and lower cost estimates. We also added an Isolation Forest anomaly detector to catch sudden cost spikes, such as forgotten servers or network leaks, before the month ends.</p>
<p>We tested our system on real-style billing datasets with 32,850 records. Our forecasting model achieved a low error rate with a Mean Absolute Percentage Error of just 3.60%. The anomaly detection model reached 99.76% overall accuracy, catching 96.68% of cost leaks with very few false alerts. By comparing instance prices and sizing, the system also found over 2.6 million dollars in possible annual savings. Overall, this project shows how simple data analytics and open standards can help teams control multi-cloud costs and avoid unexpected bills.</p>

<!-- 1. INTRODUCTION OF THE STUDY -->
<h1>1. Introduction of the Study</h1>

<h2>1.1 Background of Cloud Adoption</h2>
<p>Cloud computing has completely changed how companies build and deliver digital products. In the past, companies had to purchase physical servers, set up network cables in data centers, and wait weeks for hardware delivery. Today, any software engineer can log into a cloud console like AWS, Azure, or Google Cloud and start hundreds of virtual machines in just a few minutes. This speed has allowed startups and large enterprises to launch applications faster than ever.</p>
<p>However, this flexibility creates a serious financial problem. Because provisioning servers is so easy, engineering teams frequently spin up resources for testing or staging and forget to turn them off. Furthermore, business teams only find out about these costs at the end of the month when the cloud provider sends an invoice. Industry research shows that between 20% and 35% of all cloud spending is completely wasted on idle or unneeded resources.</p>

<h2>1.2 The Challenge of Multi-Cloud Billing</h2>
<p>Most modern businesses do not rely on a single cloud vendor. To avoid vendor lock-in and take advantage of specific provider strengths, companies adopt a multi-cloud strategy. For instance, a firm might run machine learning models on Google Cloud Platform, host business applications on Microsoft Azure, and keep production web services on Amazon Web Services.</p>
<p>While this multi-cloud approach gives operational flexibility, it creates total chaos for finance managers. Each cloud provider uses its own billing taxonomy. AWS bills compute by "EC2 Instance Hours", Azure records "Virtual Machine compute duration", and GCP bills using "Compute Engine vCPU and RAM SKUs". Trying to combine these separate CSV files in Excel takes days of manual effort, leading to errors and delays in financial reporting.</p>

<h2>1.3 Why FinOps and Machine Learning Are Needed</h2>
<p>To solve this problem, the IT industry created a practice called "FinOps" (Financial Operations). FinOps brings engineering, finance, and product teams together so that everyone takes responsibility for cloud costs. But doing FinOps manually with spreadsheets is impossible for large workloads. Automated data pipelines and machine learning algorithms are needed to clean multi-cloud records, forecast future bills accurately, and alert engineers as soon as an unusual cost spike happens.</p>

<!-- 2. OBJECTIVES OF THE STUDY -->
<h1>2. Objectives of the Study</h1>
<p>The primary objective of this research is to design and evaluate an automated multi-cloud FinOps intelligence platform that brings cost visibility, spend forecasting, and anomaly detection to modern enterprise environments across AWS, Azure, and Google Cloud Platform.</p>

<h3>Specific Objectives:</h3>
<ul>
    <li><b>Standardize Multi-Cloud Data:</b> Ingest disparate billing files from AWS CUR, Azure Cost Management, and GCP BigQuery and convert them into the unified FinOps Open Cost and Usage Specification (FOCUS 1.0) format.</li>
    <li><b>Predict Future Cloud Spending:</b> Build and calibrate a machine learning time-series forecasting model combining Fourier seasonal harmonics with gradient boosted regression to achieve a Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE) below 5%.</li>
    <li><b>Detect Cost Anomalies Automatically:</b> Implement an unsupervised Multivariate Isolation Forest algorithm to flag unexpected cost spikes, forgotten servers, and data egress storms in real-time with high recall and low false alarms.</li>
    <li><b>Formulate Actionable Rightsizing:</b> Develop an automated rule-based rightsizing engine that maps underutilized compute, unattached storage disks, and stale snapshots to cost-efficient instance families and commitment discounts.</li>
    <li><b>Provide an Accessible Control Plane:</b> Build a responsive dashboard allowing engineering and financial managers to monitor live KPIs, explore unit costs, and track optimization savings easily.</li>
</ul>

<!-- 3. LITERATURE REVIEW / BACKGROUND STUDY -->
<h1>3. Literature Review / Background Study</h1>

<h2>3.1 Evolution of Cloud Cost Management</h2>
<p>Early research in cloud computing focused mainly on system performance, low latency, and high availability. Authors like Armbrust et al. (2010) identified on-demand scalability as the primary economic advantage of cloud systems. However, as cloud environments grew larger, researchers noticed that on-demand billing easily leads to budget overruns if usage is not monitored closely. Early cost management tools relied on static threshold alerts, such as emailing an engineer when total monthly spending exceeded 80% of a budget limit. However, static rules cannot distinguish between expected business growth and wasteful resource leaks.</p>

<h2>3.2 The FOCUS 1.0 Open Standard</h2>
<p>A major hurdle in multi-cloud research has been the lack of a common data schema. The FinOps Foundation, hosted under the Linux Foundation (FinOps Foundation, 2024), launched the FinOps Open Cost and Usage Specification (FOCUS). FOCUS defines standard column names, such as "BilledCost", "EffectiveCost", "ProviderName", "ServiceName", and "PricingQuantity". Research by cloud practitioners shows that adopting FOCUS reduces the time needed to build multi-cloud reporting pipelines from months to days, creating a solid base for machine learning models.</p>

<h2>3.3 Time-Series Spend Forecasting Techniques</h2>
<p>Predicting cloud costs is tricky because cloud usage contains multiple patterns: weekly business cycles (high on weekdays, lower on weekends), monthly billing resets, and long-term business growth. Traditional linear models like ARIMA or Holt-Winters struggle when spending has non-linear spikes or sudden architectural changes. Recent studies (such as research on gradient boosted regression and hybrid Fourier decomposition) show that combining deterministic seasonal curves with non-linear tree regressors yields significantly lower error rates while providing upper and lower prediction intervals.</p>

<h2>3.4 Unsupervised Anomaly Detection with Isolation Forest</h2>
<p>Detecting billing anomalies in real enterprise workloads is challenging because real billing logs rarely have ground-truth labels indicating which line item was a mistake. Supervised machine learning models cannot be trained easily without labelled historic waste data. Liu, Ting, and Zhou (2008) introduced the Isolation Forest algorithm, which isolates anomalous data points by randomly partitioning feature dimensions. Because anomalies are few and different from normal behavior, they require far fewer random splits to isolate. Applied to cloud billing, Isolation Forest effectively flags abnormal spending without requiring manual historical tagging.</p>

