<!-- 4. RESEARCH METHODOLOGY -->
<h1>4. Research Methodology</h1>

<h2>4.1 Secondary Research Design</h2>
<p>This study relies entirely on a secondary quantitative research methodology. Rather than distributing qualitative surveys or questionnaires to employees, the research investigates empirical operational data. We examined 32,850 historical multi-cloud billing records spanning 365 continuous days, along with four detailed export files representing standard enterprise cloud architectures across AWS, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud Platform.</p>

<h2>4.2 Data Normalization Pipeline (FOCUS 1.0)</h2>
<p>The raw billing files from each cloud provider have different structures. We developed an automated ingestion pipeline that maps each vendor file into the FOCUS 1.0 standard schema:</p>
<ul>
    <li><b>AWS CUR 2.0 Mapping:</b> Mapped <code>lineItem/UnblendedCost</code> to <code>BilledCost</code>, <code>lineItem/ProductCode</code> to <code>ServiceName</code>, and <code>pricing/unit</code> to <code>PricingUnit</code>.</li>
    <li><b>Azure Cost Export Mapping:</b> Mapped <code>CostInBillingCurrency</code> to <code>BilledCost</code>, <code>MeterCategory</code> to <code>ServiceName</code>, and <code>ConsumedService</code> to <code>ProviderName</code>.</li>
    <li><b>GCP BigQuery Export Mapping:</b> Mapped <code>cost</code> + <code>credits.amount</code> to <code>EffectiveCost</code>, <code>service.description</code> to <code>ServiceName</code>, and <code>sku.description</code> to <code>SubService</code>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>4.3 Spend Forecasting Pipeline</h2>
<p>To model daily spending trends, the forecasting engine employs a multi-step design. First, a deterministic Fourier regression captures weekly cycles (7-day harmonics) and monthly cycles (30.4-day harmonics). Second, the residuals are fitted using an L2-regularized <code>HistGradientBoostingRegressor</code>. To provide risk boundaries for financial controllers, the model computes monotonic quantile bounds (P05, P50, and P95) using pinball loss, ensuring that lower and upper bounds never cross.</p>

<h2>4.4 Anomaly Detection Engine</h2>
<p>The anomaly detector runs a calibrated Multivariate Isolation Forest using daily spend, percentage deviation from the rolling 14-day median, and service-level z-scores. To prevent alert fatigue, statistical anomaly scores are paired with domain heuristic rules. For example, sudden jumps in compute cost accompanied by near-zero CPU utilization are flagged as "Zombie Compute Instances", while high daily storage fees with zero read/write operations are tagged as "Unattached Disk Leaks".</p>

<h2>4.5 Rightsizing and Optimization Logic</h2>
<p>The rightsizing engine continuously evaluates instance utilization against official hyperscaler pricing catalogs. It evaluates three optimization strategies: (1) Modern Family Migration (e.g., upgrading AWS m4 to m6i or Azure D4v3 to D4v5 for 15-20% better price-performance), (2) Burstable Downsizing (moving idle dev/test instances to burstable tiers like t4g or B-series), and (3) Commitment Plan Coverage (recommending 1-year or 3-year Savings Plans and Committed Use Discounts for baseline workloads).</p>

